    "LivenessProbe": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "v0.5 \u2014 liveness-probe declaration (internal/design/liveness.md \u00a73.3). Absent (or\n`probe=\"none\"`) \u21d2 this adapter implements no probe and the platform INFERS a status from\nconfiguration instead. Present with `probe != \"none\"` \u21d2 the adapter implements the optional\n`LivenessProbeAdapter` method and it may be called.\n\nStatically readable with no call \u2014 which is the point: a UI must be able to say \"cannot be\ntested\", or \"checking this leaves your network\", before probing anything.\n\n`probe` is a KIND, not a boolean, because the kind answers the question an operator actually\nhas before pressing a button: `local` is in-process and free; `endpoint` reaches infrastructure\nthey operate themselves; `vendor` leaves their network and may count against a rate limit.\nWhat it never means is \"billed\" \u2014 a probe is never a billed request, and a vendor with no free\nliveness call declares `none` and takes the inferred status instead (\u00a74).",
      "properties": {
        "probe": {
          "default": "none",
          "enum": [
            "none",
            "local",
            "endpoint",
            "vendor"
          ],
          "title": "Probe",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "method": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "default": null,
          "title": "Method"
        },
        "timeout_s": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "number"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "default": null,
          "title": "Timeout S"
        },
        "notes": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "default": null,
          "title": "Notes"
        }
      },
      "title": "LivenessProbe",
      "type": "object"
    },
    "LivenessReport": {
      "title": "LivenessReport",
      "description": "v0.1 \u2014 new family. One backend's liveness answer: is it configured, and if it can be probed at all, did it actually answer? Distinguishes an INFERRED state (read from local configuration, no round trip) from a MEASURED one (we contacted the backend), which is the whole point of the family \u2014 'configured' must never render as 'responding'. Produced by openreading.liveness.check_liveness and returned by POST /v1/backends/{backend_id}/liveness. Design: internal/design/liveness.md.",
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": [
        "schema_version",
        "backend",
        "status",
        "measured",
        "probe",
        "checked_at"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "schema_version": {
          "const": "0.1",
          "description": "In-band family version (Canon \u00a78 C12)."
        },
        "backend": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Registry slug, e.g. 'docling'."
        },
        "status": {
          "enum": [
            "not_supported",
            "not_configured",
            "configured_unverified",
            "live",
            "unreachable",
            "unauthorized",
            "error"
          ],
          "description": "The seven-state ladder (design \u00a72), ascending in what is known: not_configured -> configured_unverified -> live, with unreachable/unauthorized/error as the MEASURED negatives and not_supported as the floor. 'not_supported' = the adapter declares no probe AND declares no credential/config field, so there is nothing to infer from either. 'not_configured' = a required credential/config var is missing; it is a known negative and always beats an optimistic inference, and it short-circuits the probe rather than spending a call. 'configured_unverified' = no probe is available, but every declared requirement resolves \u2014 an INFERENCE from local configuration, never a measurement. 'live' = we called it and it answered healthy. 'unreachable' = nothing answered (refused/DNS/timeout). 'unauthorized' = it answered and rejected the credential. 'error' = it answered but not in a way that proves health, or the probe itself raised."
        },
        "measured": {
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "Did a real round trip to the backend happen? True for live/unreachable/unauthorized/error; false for not_supported/not_configured/configured_unverified. Derivable from `status`, and carried explicitly so a third-party consumer can tell a measurement from an inference without encoding this enum's semantics \u2014 and so it stays correct if the ladder grows a state."
        },
        "probe": {
          "enum": [
            "none",
            "local",
            "endpoint",
            "vendor"
          ],
          "description": "The backend's STATIC probe declaration (descriptor.liveness.probe), echoed so a consumer needs no second lookup. 'none' = not probeable. 'local' = in-process/subprocess, no network. 'endpoint' = network, to infrastructure the CALLER operates (a self-hosted container or model server) \u2014 does not leave their network. 'vendor' = network, to a third party \u2014 it does. A probe is NEVER a billed request in any kind."
        },
        "checked_at": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "When THIS report was produced, ISO-8601 UTC (e.g. '2026-08-17T09:41:07Z'). For a measured report that is when the backend was contacted; for an inferred one it is when the environment was read \u2014 it never implies a round trip that did not happen."
        },
        "latency_ms": {
          "type": [
            "number",
            "null"
          ],
          "minimum": 0,
          "description": "Round-trip time of the probe. ALWAYS null when `measured` is false \u2014 fabricating a latency for an inferred state would imply a call that was never made."
        },
        "detail": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "One short human sentence: what was concluded and what to do about it. NEVER carries a secret (redacted on the way out), never a provider's raw response body (providers have been seen echoing a rejected key back inside one), and never an endpoint URL (a URL can embed credentials) \u2014 env var NAMES only, matching the openreading.credentials docstring's posture."
        },
        "version": {
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ],
          "description": "Any version or model identifier the probe learned (e.g. the served model id from an OpenAI-compatible /v1/models). Null when the probe yields none."
        }
      }
    },
    "LivenessRequest": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "backend": {
          "pattern": "^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$",
          "title": "Backend",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "timeout_s": {
          "default": 5.0,
          "maximum": 30,
          "minimum": 0.1,
          "title": "Timeout S",
          "type": "number"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "backend"
      ],
      "title": "LivenessRequest",
      "type": "object"
    },
    "LivenessStatus": {
      "description": "The seven-state ladder (internal/design/liveness.md \u00a72), in ascending order of what is known.\n\n`not_configured` \u2192 `configured_unverified` \u2192 `live`, with `unreachable`/`unauthorized`/`error`\nas the measured negatives and `not_supported` as the \"nothing to say\" floor.\n\nThe load-bearing distinction is `configured_unverified` (INFERRED from a local config read \u2014\nno round trip happened) versus `live` (MEASURED \u2014 we called it and it answered). Rendering\nthose two the same way reintroduces the exact defect this ladder exists to fix: \"configured\"\nwearing the word \"ready\". `LivenessReport.measured` states which side a report is on without a\nconsumer having to encode this enum's semantics.",
      "enum": [
        "not_supported",
        "not_configured",
        "configured_unverified",
        "live",
        "unreachable",
        "unauthorized",
        "error"
      ],
      "title": "LivenessStatus",
      "type": "string"
    },
